course: dictionary
lesson: 7054
title: Heat that keeps food safe
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Scientists give a name to a process so everyone can talk about it. Look at the clues around the word. «From the farm the milk travels cold in a tanker to the dairy, where a quick heating called pasteurisation kills the germs.» The words "a quick heating" and "kills the germs" tell you what pasteurisation does.
explain: The second sentence gives more detail: «Milk is cooled to about 4 degrees straight after milking, and at the dairy it is heated to about 72 degrees for about 15 seconds; this pasteurisation, named after Louis Pasteur, kills germs.» So pasteurisation is heating for a short time, then cooling. Check it by swapping your meaning in: "this heating, named after Louis Pasteur, kills germs" still makes sense.
explain: Pasteurisation is a noun, and its verb is pasteurise. Pasteurised milk is milk that has been through this process. In US and international spelling it is «pasteurization».
fact: Pasteurisation means heating food to a set temperature for a short time to kill harmful germs, then cooling it quickly.
fact: It is named after the French scientist Louis Pasteur, who showed that tiny microbes make food go bad and cause disease.
fact: Pasteurisation is not the same as boiling: the milk is heated less, so it still tastes like milk.
fact: Pasteurised milk lasts longer in the fridge and is much less likely to make you ill.
fact: «Pasteurisation» is a noun, «pasteurise» is the verb, and «pasteurization» is the US and international spelling.
exercise: What does pasteurisation mean? | Boiling food until it is very hot for hours | *Heating food to a set temperature for a short time to kill harmful germs, then cooling it quickly | Adding sugar to food to keep it sweet | Freezing food so that nothing can grow in it
exercise: Who is pasteurisation named after? | *Louis Pasteur, a French scientist | Isaac Newton | Albert Einstein | Charles Darwin
exercise: Why is pasteurised milk safer to drink? | *Heating it kills harmful germs that can make you ill | It is coloured white | It contains extra sugar | It has been boiled until it is thick
test:
Which word is the verb form? | Pasteurisation | *Pasteurise | Pasteur | Germ
Why does pasteurised milk still taste like milk? | *Because it is heated less than boiling | Because it is boiled for hours | Because it is left out in the sun | Because it is frozen solid
exam:
Which spelling is used in US and international English? | pasteurisation | *pasteurization | pastureisation | pasteure
